            <ArticleTitle>Recent examples on the clinical relevance of the CYP2D6 polymorphism and endogenous functionality of CYP2D6.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>209-16</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/dmdi-2013-0032</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/dmdi.2013.28.issue-4/dmdi-2013-0032/dmdi-2013-0032.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The cytochrome P450 2D6 (CYP2D6) belongs to a group of CYPs considered of utmost importance in the metabolism of xenobiotics. Despite being of only minor abundance in the liver, it is involved in the clearance of &gt;25% of marketed drugs. Accordingly, CYP2D6 can be very efficiently inhibited by a couple of commonly used drugs such as some antidepressants, although induction by any drug has not been observed thus far. CYP2D6 was also one of the first enzymes for which a highly polymorphic expression could be shown leading to a widespread range of functionality, from a complete lack of a functional enzyme to overexpression due to multiplication of active alleles. A clear relationship between the CYP2D6 genotype and adverse events during treatment with CNS-active drugs such as codeine, antidepressants, or antipsychotics could be demonstrated. More recently, some new aspects emerged about the potential endogenous function of CYP2D6 in terms of behavior and brain disorders.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
